        "<p>I_jt = I_j,t−1 + Σ_i(y_ij q_it) − contract_j − s_jt.</p>",
        "<p>The sum adds production from all crude types.</p>",
        "<h2>Constraints</h2>",
        "<p>All quantities are nonnegative. Daily feed is at most 20,000 barrels; weighted workload is at most the selected limit. Onshore crude before production is at most 800,000 barrels. Each product tank before contract shipment is at most 120,000 barrels. Daily gasoline and diesel contracts are 5,000 barrels each.</p>",
        "<p>Extra sales occur every five days, capped at 5,000 gasoline, 15,000 diesel and 30,000 residue barrels. Before day 60, sale-day closing gasoline/diesel inventory must cover the next day&#x27;s 5,000-barrel contracts. LP shares these constraints but may choose feed proportions and reduce daily runs.</p>",
        "<h2>Profit and terminal value</h2>",
        "<p>Modeled profit = 60-day sales − purchase, freight, unloading, processing, storage and shipping costs + closing inventory value − opening inventory value.</p>",
        "<p>After day 60, production and contracts end. Products are liquidated from day 65 at five-day intervals, within sales caps and at base prices, less future storage and shipping. The LP uses 24 opportunities, enough for a full gasoline tank.</p>",
        "<p>Remaining crude is valued at acquisition cost plus freight. The dashboard also includes the allocated remaining unloading cost in onshore crude value. The common LP comparison excludes that value from both methods; actual unloading expenditure remains a cost. Subtracting opening value prevents existing assets from being counted as newly earned profit.</p>",
